3. Web App — Turbo Mode

Turbo Mode is a lightweight, high-velocity alternative optimized for the rapid screening of large-scale experiments, such as 96-well or 384-well plates.

3.1 When to Use Turbo Mode

While the Standard Mode uses a rigorous "glocal" alignment for every read, Turbo Mode utilizes a heuristic alignment algorithm. This allows for massive throughput, though it may be slightly less precise when calling complex, non-standard variants.

Metric	Standard Mode	Turbo Mode
Throughput	High Precision (~10K reads/sec)	Extreme Speed (~100K reads/sec)
Logic	Exhaustive Glocal Alignment	Heuristic (Short-cut) Mapping
Best For	Final data for publication	Initial high-volume screening
Visuals	Full alignment + detailed plots	Simplified allele tables


3.2 Setup

1. Paste your Reference Sequence
2. Enter the gRNA Sequence (optional — for highlighting)
3. Upload one or more FASTQ files (or paste sequences)
4. Click "Run Analysis"

3.3 96-Well Plate View

Results are displayed as a 96-well plate grid (A1–H12):

- Each well shows a pie chart (conic gradient) representing the allele distribution
- Color legend (shown above the plate):
  - 🟢 Green — Wildtype
  - 🟣 Purple — HDR
  - 🔵 Blue — In-Frame Indel
  - 🔴 Red — Out-of-Frame Indel
  - 🟡 Amber — Substitution/SNP

Dimmed Wells
Wells with fewer than 100 aligned reads appear dimmed (30% opacity, dashed border). This indicates insufficient data for reliable genotyping. Hover over a dimmed well to see the exact read count.

3.4 Well Details

Click any well to view its detailed Allele Breakdown below the plate:

- Min Reads filter (default: 100) — Hides rare alleles below this threshold. Adjustable per-well.
- Allele table — Shows each unique genotype with alignment, type, delta, and read count
- Indel size distribution chart

3.5 File Naming for Multiwell

For automatic well assignment, name your files with the well position:
- `A1_VHL.fastq`, `B3-sample.fq`, `C12.fastq`
- Pattern: `[A-H][1-12]` at the start of the filename
- Files without well positions are assigned sequentially (A1, A2, ..., H12)

4. Python CLI

The Python CLI (`cleanfinder.py`) provides the same analysis engine in a command-line interface, suitable for batch processing and integration into bioinformatics pipelines.

4.1 Installation

Requirements: Python 3.7+ (no external dependencies — uses only the standard library)

No Installation Required. CleanFinder is a standalone script that uses only the Python Standard Library.

4.2 Basic Usage and Arguments

The primary workflow requires a **Reference** (FASTA or plain text) and Reads (one or more FASTQ files).

bash
Standard command structure
python cleanfinder.py -r [REFERENCE] -q [READS.fastq] -g [GRNA]

Example: Single file analysis
python cleanfinder.py -r ref.fa -q sample_A1.fastq -g TCTGCAGAATGTCAAAG

4.3 Full Command-Line Reference

Required (unless using batch mode)

| Flag | Description |
|---|---|
| `-r`, `--ref` | Reference sequence — FASTA file or plain text sequence |
| `-q`, `--reads` | FASTQ file(s) with sequencing reads (supports multiple files) |

Optional

| Category | Flag | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Input** | `-r`, `--ref` | — | **(Required)** Reference sequence (FASTA file or plain DNA string) |
| | `-q`, `--reads` | — | **(Required)** FASTQ file(s). Supports multiple space-separated files |
| | `-g`, `--grna` | — | gRNA sequence (without PAM) for window anchoring |
| **Logic** | `--mode` | `cas9` | Analysis mode: `cas9`, `cas12`, `prime`, `base` |
| | `--hdr` | — | Expected HDR/Knock-in template sequence |
| | `--pe-edit` | — | Expected Prime Edit sequence (required for `prime` mode) |
| | `--be-conversion`| — | Base conversion pattern, e.g., `C>T` (required for `base` mode) |
| **Filters** | `--min-phred` | `0` | Minimum average Phred score per read (0–40) |
| | `--max-mismatches`| `2` | Max mismatches allowed in anchor regions (default: 2) |
| | `--anchor-len` | `20` | Length of flanking anchors (default: 20 bp) |
| | `--window-size` | `40` | Window size on each side of gRNA (default: 40 bp) |
| **Output**| `-o`, `--output` | `results.csv`| Path for the output CSV (or directory for batch mode) |
| | `--top` | `10` | Number of top alleles to display in the console |
| | `--no-alignment` | — | Disable alignment visualization in the console |
| | `--quiet` | — | Print only summary stats (useful for large batches) |
| **Batch** | `--batch` | — | Path to a CSV config file for processing diverse samples |

4.4 Analysis Modes — Examples

A. Standard Cas9 / Cas12a
Standard indel analysis. Cas12 mode allows for the characteristic staggered cut sites.
```bash
python cleanfinder.py -r ref.fa -q reads.fq -g ATCGATCGATCGATCG --mode cas9
```

B. Prime Editing (PE)
Analyzes precise swaps/insertions. Requires the expected edit sequence.
```bash
python cleanfinder.py -r ref.fa -q reads.fq --mode prime --pe-edit GCTAGCTAG --window-size 60
```

C. Base Editing (BE)
Focuses on substitutions and suppresses indel calling via high gap penalties.
```bash
python cleanfinder.py -r ref.fa -q reads.fq --mode base --be-conversion C>T
```

D. Quality-Restricted Analysis
Filter out low-quality reads (e.g., from ONT runs) to prevent false mutation calls.
```bash
python cleanfinder.py -r ref.fa -q sample.fq --min-phred 25 --top 20
```

4.5 Multi-File Analysis and Processing

CleanFinder can process multiple files simultaneously if they share the same reference and gRNA. This is ideal for screening 96-well plates.

```bash
# Analyze all FASTQ files in current directory
python cleanfinder.py -r ref.fa -g GRNA -q *.fastq -o summary.csv
```

Output:
1. A detailed report for every individual file.
2. A **Multiwell Summary Table** at the very end comparing Editing Efficiency across all files.

