5. Allelic Dropout Analyzer

The Allelic Dropout (ADO) Analyzer (`allelic_dropout.html`) is a specialized tool designed to detect heterozygous SNPs in long-read sequencing data. This is critical for assessing whether CRISPR editing has caused a "dropout" of one allele (e.g., due to large deletions or rearrangements).

5.1 Interface Overview

The interface is optimized for targeted SNP detection:
- Input Panel — FASTQ file upload, anchor sequence definition, and reference configuration.
- Fetch Tool — Automated retrieval of genomic sequences from Ensembl/UCSC by gene symbol.
- Parameters Panel — Controls for scan window size, coverage thresholds, and allele frequency ranges.
- Results Dashboard — Clear "BIALLELIC" or "MONOALLELIC" calls with detailed SNP analysis.

5.2 Setting Up an Analysis

Loading Data and Reference
1. Upload FASTQ — Select your sequencing file (optimized for long-read data).
2. Stable Anchor Sequence — Enter a 15–20bp unique sequence present in all reads. This must be a "stable" region far from the expected editing site/SNP.
3. Reference Sequence — Paste your wildtype amplicon sequence or use the Fetch from Gene Symbol tool.
   - Tip: Providing a gRNA sequence during fetch ensures the reference is perfectly centered on your region of interest.

Tuning Parameters
- Scan Window — How many bases to search on either side of the anchor (Default: 500bp).
- Min Coverage — Minimum reads required to call a SNP (Default: 500).
- Min/Max Allele Freq — The frequency range expected for a heterozygous SNP (Default: 30%–70%).

5.3 Running the Analysis

1. Click "Run Analysis".
2. Matrix Construction — The tool aligns reads around your stable anchor and builds a base-frequency matrix for every position in the scan window.
3. SNP Detection — High-quality positions showing two distinct bases within the target frequency range are identified.

5.4 Understanding ADO Results

| Call | Meaning |
|---|---|
| BIALLELIC | (🟢 Green) A high-confidence heterozygous SNP was detected. Both alleles are present in the sample. |
| MONOALLELIC | (🟡 Amber) No heterozygous SNPs were found. This may indicate Allelic Dropout or a naturally homozygous sample. |
| ERROR | (🔴 Red) Analysis failed, likely due to low anchor matching or insufficient coverage. |

SNP Table Details
- Position — Relative to your anchor sequence.
- Bases & Frequencies — The identity and ratio of the two observed alleles.
- Context — A visual snippet of the reference sequence around the identified SNP.

6. Technical Notes

6.1 Analysis Pipeline

The analysis follows these steps for each read:

```
Read → Anchor Matching → Middle Extraction → Classification → Alignment
```

1. Anchor Matching — The left and right flanking sequences (anchors) are searched in the read using fuzzy matching (allowing up to N mismatches). Both forward and reverse-complement orientations are tested.
2. Middle Extraction — The region between the two anchors is extracted. This is the "editing window" compared against the reference.
3. Classification — The extracted middle is compared to the reference middle to determine the genotype.
4. Alignment — An affine gap fitting alignment is performed to visualize the exact mutations.

6.2 Anchor Matching

Anchors are the first and last N bases of the reference sequence (default: 20 bp). They serve as landmarks to locate the editing window in each read.

- Fuzzy matching allows up to `max_mismatches` (default: 2) mismatches per anchor
- Both forward and **reverse complement** orientations are tested
- Reads where neither orientation matches are classified as "Unaligned"

6.3 Classification Logic

| Type | Condition |
|---|---|
| WT (Wildtype) | Middle sequence exactly matches reference |
| KI (Knock-In) | HDR template found within the middle sequence |
| PE (Prime Edit) | PE edit sequence found within the middle (Prime Editing mode) |
| BE (Base Edit) | Expected base conversions detected with no indels (Base Editing mode) |
| SNP | Same length as reference but different sequence |
| DEL (Deletion) | Shorter than reference (Δ < 0) |
| INS (Insertion) | Longer than reference (Δ > 0) |

6.4 Frame Detection

For indels (DEL and INS), frame status is determined by the delta:

- In-Frame — Δ is divisible by 3 (preserves reading frame)
- Out-of-Frame — Δ is not divisible by 3 (disrupts reading frame)

6.5 Alignment Algorithm

CleanFinder uses affine gap fitting alignment with three DP states:

- M — Match/mismatch state
- Ix — Gap in query (deletion in read)
- Iy — Gap in reference (insertion in read)

Default scoring parameters vary by mode:

| Parameter | Cas9/Cas12 | Prime Editing | Base Editing |
|---|---|---|---|
| Match | +2 | +2 | +2 |
| Mismatch | -6 | -3 | -6 |
| Gap Open | -10 | -1 | -20 |
| Gap Extend | -2 | -0.5 | -20 |

- Cas9/Cas12 — Standard penalties, good for detecting indels
- Prime Editing — Relaxed gap penalties to detect precise small edits
- Base Editing — Very high gap penalties to avoid calling indels; focuses on substitutions

6.6 Microhomology Detection

For deletion events, CleanFinder detects microhomology at the deletion junction, which indicates the repair pathway:

- MMEJ (Microhomology-Mediated End Joining) — ≥2 bp microhomology at the junction
- NHEJ (Non-Homologous End Joining) — <2 bp microhomology

This is detected by comparing the sequence flanking the deletion breakpoint for shared motifs.

7. FAQ & Troubleshooting

Low alignment rate

Problem: Most reads are "unaligned" or "unclassified."

Solutions:
- Check that the reference sequence matches your actual amplicon
- Increase Max Mismatches (e.g., from 2 to 3)
- Reduce Anchor Length (e.g., from 20 to 15) for shorter amplicons
- Verify your reads are in the correct orientation

No reads found in FASTQ

Problem: The app reports 0 reads.

Solutions:
- Ensure the file is in standard FASTQ format (4 lines per record: @header, sequence, +, quality)
- Check the file is not gzipped (`.fastq.gz` is not supported — decompress first)

All reads classified as WT

Problem: 100% wildtype even in edited samples.

Solutions:
- Verify the gRNA sequence is correct
- Check that the analysis window covers the cut site (adjust Window Size)
- Ensure the reference matches the actual wildtype amplicon (not a mutant)

Turbo Mode: all pie charts look the same

Problem: Wells in the plate view all show identical proportions.

Solutions:
- This is usually correct — if you targeted the same gene, many wells may have similar editing profiles
- Wells with <100 reads are dimmed to indicate insufficient data
- Click individual wells to see the detailed allele breakdown

Large files are slow

Solutions:
- Use Turbo Mode for quick screening of many samples
- In Standard Mode, consider processing one file at a time for very large files
- Apply Phred quality filtering to reduce read count

CSV export is empty

Problem: Downloaded CSV has only headers.

Solutions:
- Ensure the analysis completed successfully (check for error messages)
- Verify reads were aligned (check the "Aligned" count in the summary)

